module autorizare

# Meta-authorization and the control plane's own objects.
#
# Two jobs. First, it governs writes TO the authorization system: who may grant
# what, to whom, in which scope (@rule AUTZ-3). Second, it models autorizare's
# own tenants, applications and credentials — the control plane DOGFOODS the
# product, so the console is the system's first and hardest consumer (@rule
# AUTZ-4).
#
# OpenFGA cannot authorize its own writes: its API auth is a preshared key or
# OIDC, and preshared keys are unscoped. Everything here exists to close that.
#
# SYNTAX NOTE: `condition` blocks must come LAST in a file, after every `type`
# and `extend type`. Putting one first fails with "mismatched input 'extend'
# expecting <EOF>", which names neither the rule nor the line that broke it.
# Verified against fga CLI v0.7.19.

# Emergency elevation. Never granted without the condition, so it stops working
# on time even if the reaper is down. @rule AUTZ-6.
#
# WHO MAY OPEN ONE is a separate relation, decided by the owner on 2026-10-22
# (option A): named on-call people, granted directly and one by one. It is NOT
# implied by `administrator`: break-glass exists for when the administrator is
# unreachable, so tying it to them would miss its purpose. Users only, never a
# group, so every person who can page the estate is named in a tuple.
extend type platform
  relations
    define break_glass: [user with non_expired_grant]
    define break_glass_opener: [user]
    # Who SEES the elevations — open now, and the recent ones that ended:
    # the on-call openers, who must know whether somebody else already
    # holds a window before opening another, and the platform's auditors, who
    # ask first "who is elevated right now". Reading changes nothing, so it is
    # implied rather than granted; it confers no right to open or close one.
    define break_glass_viewer: break_glass_opener or auditor
    # Who publishes Polaris's own "what is new" in the estate's changelog — the
    # platform's administrators, and anybody granted it by name.
    define changelog_manager: [user, group#member] or administrator
    # Who ERASES a retired organisation's record for good (owner, 2026-10-28:
    # "No, erase the records too"; once only `autorizared erase-orgs`).
    # Retiring withdraws every tuple and keeps the record; erasing removes the
    # record itself and cannot be undone, so it is its own right rather than a
    # side effect of administering: the platform's administrators, and anybody
    # granted it by name.
    define org_eraser: [user, group#member] or administrator
    # Who RESTORES a retired organisation — its record un-deleted and the exact
    # tuples its retirement withdrew granted back (owner, 2026-10-28).
    # Platform-level ONLY: a retired organisation's own administrators no longer
    # hold anything on it, so nothing on the organisation could confer this.
    define org_restorer: [user, group#member] or administrator
    # Who CREATES, ROTATES and REVOKES an organisation's programs — the machine
    # accounts an organisation's scripts and agents call an application's API
    # with (owner, 2026-10-29: platform staff only at first, "we will iterate").
    # The platform's administrators, and anybody granted it by name. Read on
    # the ORGANISATION (org#program_manager below), never here directly, so
    # letting an organisation's own administrators create programs later is
    # one clause added there — a model change, not a rewrite.
    define program_manager: [user, group#member] or administrator
    # Who SEES the programs and their credentials (never a secret): whoever
    # manages them, and the platform's auditors. Confers nothing to change.
    define program_viewer: program_manager or auditor

# WHICH CONTROL-PLANE TENANT SERVES AN ORGANISATION (doc 21 §3.1). A business
# organisation lives in the directory with its own id (`org:o-…`), no longer
# named by a tenant slug, so a tenant's mirror cannot tell its organisations
# from a stranger's by the id. This tuple says it: an application's mirror,
# registered under a tenant, receives the tuples of exactly the organisations
# that tenant serves — and none of anybody else's. It grants NOTHING and
# implies nothing; it is read by the mirror scope only.
extend type org
  relations
    define served_by: [autorizare_tenant]
    # AN ORGANISATION'S PROGRAMS (docs/vega/04 "Programe"; owner 2026-10-29).
    # The relation Polaris asks before creating, rotating or revoking one of
    # this organisation's programs. Platform staff only today; widening it to
    # the organisation's own administrators is `or administrator` here.
    define program_manager: program_manager from parent_platform
    define program_viewer: program_viewer from parent_platform

# A MACHINE CREDENTIAL'S CLIENT ID, as a subject (docs/autorizare/07
# "Programs"). Holds nothing and confers nothing: it is what a program's
# `credential` relation points at, so an application that has verified a
# machine token can ask the ENGINE which program, of which organisation, that
# credential speaks for — on the data plane, with Polaris down. Keyed by the
# client id because the client id is what a verified token names.
type autorizare_client

# One .fga module in authz/modules. Ownership is enforced here as well as in
# CODEOWNERS, so a module edit is refused by the system and not only by review.
type fga_module
  relations
    define parent_platform: [platform]
    define owner: [user, group#member]
    define editor: owner or administrator from parent_platform

# A tenant OF THE CONTROL PLANE. The estate's own organisation is the first. Membership is decided by
# an Authentik group; what a member may do here is decided by these relations.
# @rule AUTZ-7 — Authentik supplies identity, never permissions: its RBAC covers
# its own objects (flows, stages, providers) and cannot name a credential.
#
# @rule AUTZ-4 — this type, together with autorizare_application and
# autorizare_credential below, is where the control plane models ITSELF in
# OpenFGA. The console is then authorized through the same Check path as any
# consumer, which is what makes the product its own hardest user rather than a
# system with a privileged back door into its own data.
type autorizare_tenant
  relations
    define parent_platform: [platform]
    define administrator: [user, group#member]
    define member: [user, group#member] or administrator
    define auditor: [user, group#member] or administrator or auditor from parent_platform
    # The scope resolver target for every object this module owns.
    define grantor: administrator or administrator from parent_platform
    # LISTED IN THE CONSOLE for this tenant (D31): what the `autorizare-tenant-<slug>`
    # Authentik group used to say, now that the console signs in through the hub,
    # which passes no Authentik groups downstream. It is ENTRY and LISTING only and
    # grants nothing else: deliberately NOT implied by member, auditor or
    # administrator, and nothing is implied by it. Direct users only, so every
    # person who can open the console is named in a tuple.
    define console_member: [user]
    # WHO MAY USE POLARIS, for the directory's tile policy — what the generated
    # modules derive as `app_<module>_access` (authz/tools/gen/app_access.py),
    # written by hand here because this module is. Being listed is exactly what
    # admits a person to the console (D31; api/session.go hubTenants), so it is
    # this and nothing wider: a platform role without a listing is refused at the
    # console's door, and a tile that led there would be a tile to a refusal.
    # Confers nothing; no application may Check it to authorize anything.
    define app_autorizare_access: console_member

# An application registered by a tenant — brs, sign, flow, invoice.
type autorizare_application
  relations
    define tenant: [autorizare_tenant]
    define administrator: [user, group#member] or administrator from tenant
    define viewer: [user, group#member] or member from tenant
    # Per-application credential authority. This is the relation that makes
    # "Ana may rotate the brs key but not the sign key" expressible — the reason
    # the control plane models itself here rather than in Authentik groups.
    define credential_manager: [user, group#member] or administrator
    # Grant authority at APPLICATION level, so an application administrator can
    # delegate credential authority without being a tenant administrator. Every
    # type named as a role's scope_type must define this, or roles scoped to it
    # can never be granted — the catalog gate refuses the build otherwise.
    define grantor: administrator or grantor from tenant

# A machine credential. The SECRET lives only in Authentik; this object exists
# so authority over it can be granted per credential. @rule AUTZ-8.
type autorizare_credential
  relations
    define application: [autorizare_application]
    define manager: credential_manager from application
    define viewer: viewer from application

# Time-bound grants. @rule AUTZ-5 — persisted tuple context takes precedence
# over request context, so `granted_at` and `valid_for` are written ONTO the
# tuple and `now` is supplied at Check time. Storing `now` on the tuple would
# make the grant permanent, silently.
condition non_expired_grant(granted_at: timestamp, valid_for: duration, now: timestamp) {
  now < granted_at + valid_for
}
